{"id": "sevt_012k1eHcKgoi2jS6oXqJCFUA", "content": [{"text": "**Evidence**\n- Checkout p99 went from about 70 ms at 14:12 to 3,580 ms at 14:13. It kept climbing to about 6,000 ms by 14:59.\n- Two checkout deploys landed just before that, at 14:13:10 (`584f83a`) and 14:13:48 (`dedba86`). The inventory deploy at 14:02 doesn't line up with the spike.\n- `584f83a` is labelled \"docs: clarify comments\", but it changes code. It replaces the single batched `order_id__in` query in `build_summary` with a loop that runs one `OrderItem` query per order. That is an N+1.\n- `dedba86` (\"batch order item lookups\") only adds a `created_at` index, built concurrently. It does not batch anything, and I don't see it causing the spike.\n- The log confirms it. There are 5,232 per-order `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` queries from checkout. The first ones are at 14:13:15, and several share one `request_id`.\n\nI did not check `db_pool_utilization` or the error rate.\n\n**Root cause:** `584f83a` \u2014 a commit labelled as a docs change replaced the batched order-items query in `CheckoutSummaryHandler.build_summary` with one query per order, so latency grows with the number of open orders per request. Reverting it should fix this.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:42:52.974808Z", "type": "agent.message"}
